  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nLingering shower activity should dissipate and shift east this\nafternoon with the cold front passage. Cloud deck should\ncontinue to scatter as shortwave ridging briefly builds\noverhead. Prevailing s-sw flow ahead of the approaching upper\ntrough should maintain temperatures above seasonal averages \ntonight and Friday.\n\nAnother round of showers are possible (low chance) as the upper\ntrough pivots southeastward over the lower Great Lakes and \nthe reinforcing cold front crosses late Friday/Fri Night. The \ndeepest moisture generally remains closer to the lakes, but some\nmoisture depth in the boundary layer and favorable wind \ndirection for upslope could present a period for showers or \ndrizzle overnight into Saturday. Given the encroaching cold \ntemps (850hpa temps approaching -2C), there is some limited \npotential for snowflakes, though given the generally lack of \nmoisture depth below, this may be cold drizzle vs snow. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nSeasonably cold temperatures are expected through the weekend as\nthe trough shifts to the east and is replaced by building high\npressure. Guidance has nighttime temperatures approaching\nfreezing Saturday, Sunday, and Monday morning, with the\npotential coldest temps Monday as the ridge of high pressure\nsettles over the region. Our counties that continue to have an\nactive growing season west of Charleston have the most likely \nchance to see a temps below 32 (>75%) Sunday into Monday, but\nfrost could impact many of the counties that continue to have \nan active growing season.",
